## 4. Scalability Analysis

### Our Architecture: Multi-Module Monorepo on Supabase

```
Single Next.js App (App Router)
  ├── All modules in one codebase
  ├── One Supabase project (Postgres)
  ├── Shared auth.users + profiles
  └── Row Level Security per employee
```

### Is This Good? Comparison Table

| Criteria | Our Approach | Pure Microservices | Separate Apps |
|----------|--------------|--------------------|---------------|
| **Setup complexity** | ✅ Low | ❌ Very High | ❌ Medium |
| **Data consistency** | ✅ Single DB, no sync | ⚠️ Hard to sync | ❌ Duplicate data |
| **Cross-module queries** | ✅ One SQL JOIN | ❌ API calls needed | ❌ Impossible |
| **Shared auth** | ✅ One Supabase Auth | ⚠️ Needs identity federation | ❌ Multiple logins |
| **Team of 1-50 devs** | ✅ Perfect | ❌ Overkill | ❌ Messy |
| **Scaling to 10k users** | ✅ Fine (Supabase scales) | ✅ Better | ⚠️ Hard |
| **Deploy complexity** | ✅ One command | ❌ Kubernetes needed | ❌ Many deploys |
| **Feature flags / RBAC** | ✅ module_access table | ⚠️ API gateway | ❌ Hard |
| **Cost** | ✅ One Supabase plan | ❌ Many services | ❌ Multiple hosting |
| **Real-time updates** | ✅ Supabase Realtime | ⚠️ Custom setup | ❌ Hard |

### What Real Tech Companies Do

| Company Size | Typical Architecture |
|---|---|
| **Startup (1–50 employees)** | Monorepo monolith — exactly what we're building |
| **Growing (50–500 employees)** | Monorepo with module boundaries (our approach) |
| **Enterprise (500–10k)** | Modular monolith → selective microservices |
| **Big Tech (Google, Amazon)** | Full microservices — 1000s of engineers needed to maintain |

> **Verdict: Our approach is the industry-standard choice for a 10–500 person tech company.** Tools like Linear, Notion, Basecamp, and Freshworks all started with exactly this architecture. Supabase + Next.js is the modern consensus stack for this scale.

### Where Our Architecture Particularly Shines

1. **RLS in Postgres** = Security enforced at DB layer, not just app layer. Can't accidentally leak data even if there's a frontend bug.

2. **Single `employees` table** = Employee ID is the foreign key in every module. HR creates one record and it propagates everywhere. No syncing needed.

3. **`module_access` table** = One row controls all module permissions. Toggling access is one SQL update — instantly reflected everywhere.

4. **`system_audit_log`** = Every action across all modules is traceable. Full GDPR compliance and internal accountability — a must for a company managing payroll and performance reviews.

5. **Supabase Realtime** = Attendance check-in, leave status updates, task changes can all be live without any WebSocket infrastructure to maintain.

### Potential Future Scale Points

| When | What to Do |
|---|---|
| DB gets slow (100k+ rows) | Add table partitioning on `attendance` by year-month |
| Payroll computation is heavy | Move payroll calculation to a Supabase Edge Function (serverless) |
| File storage is large | Switch from Supabase Storage to dedicated S3 / R2 bucket |
| Need separate teams per module | Split into Next.js route groups with module-specific DB schemas |
| 1000+ employees | Add connection pooling (PgBouncer — already built into Supabase) |

